  "spans": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "nodeId": "n3",
      "text": "Most everyone has heard the inspiring education statistic, \"We remember 10% of what we hear, 20% of what we read, 50% of what we do, 75% of what we discuss and 90% of what we teach.\"",
      "contextBefore": " my TIP of the Month! a resource for teachers & families a resource for teachers & families Philosophy | Tips for Teaching Art Going for the 90%. ByCheryl Trowbridge Published Last Updated 0 Comments ",
      "contextAfter": " Well surprisingly, I learned there is actually no scientific basis for these percentages! (For one example, check out the article: \"Debunk This: People Remember 10% of What They Read\".) Even so, I be",
      "start": 219,
      "end": 401,
      "extractSha256": "206dbec8b62cc4ff38b1635d1a6212fa50762c66bfe7712067dec118ab7b93ab",
      "windowSha256": "8f6a4109f35bb7099a0a5af3c9c98298bc1a217a07b2deede0febd52f8f38c13",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-19T17:35:50.513Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20130622035835/https://www.teachkidsart.net/going-for-90",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "nodeId": "n4",
      "text": "Typical examples claim that people remember 10% of what they read, 20% of what they hear, 50% of what they discuss, and as much as 90% of what they teach to others.",
      "contextBefore": "one. The Cone Of Learning Myth: Are Retention Percentages Real? The most widely recognized version of Dale's Cone of Learning includes retention percentages attached to different learning activities. ",
      "contextAfter": " These figures have appeared in countless presentations, training manuals, conference materials, and educational resources. Despite their popularity, there is no credible evidence supporting these pre",
      "start": 8477,
      "end": 8641,
      "extractSha256": "8f1220f4cfa0e6593f67b1e7c0525044a6a37ee224958068f74a85ec743457f1",
      "windowSha256": "eb5d2a9dc0289d3ce3a09eccb19d70cdce0760406259f9fd11249cf34b958fd3",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-19T17:35:51.654Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20190519035240/https://elearningindustry.com/cone-of-experience-what-really-is",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "nodeId": "n5",
      "text": "However eating too much pie is OK because the sin of pi is always zero.\" - copied We remember: 10% of what we hear, 30% of what we read, 50% of what we see, 90% of what we do Prostitutes are in no danger of finding their present life so satisfactory that they cannot turn to God: the proud, the avaricious, the self-righteous, are in that danger. -- C.S.",
